# ReScaleFrame settings.
#
# Copy this beside the proxy, as ReScaleFrame.ini next to Ace7Game.exe, and edit it there. It is
# read once when the proxy attaches. One NAME=value per line; # or ; starts a comment.
#
# These are the same names that used to be Steam launch options, and an environment variable still
# wins over the file, so an existing launch line keeps working and a one-off override does not mean
# editing this.
#
# Numbers accept hexadecimal with an 0x prefix, and zero means zero rather than "unset".

# Watch the game's D3D11 use. Nothing else works without it.
RSF_OBSERVE=1

# Run the engine's own temporal jitter, which every super resolution backend needs. This steps over
# the anti-aliasing gate rather than turning temporal AA on, because AC7 runs none.
#
# 1 opens the gate only while a reconstruction is resolving frames, which is what keeps the front
# end still: an offset that nothing accumulates is visible as a shimmer, and the menu holds still
# so it has nowhere to hide. 2 opens it from decryption to exit, which is what every measurement
# before this was taken under. 0 never patches.
#
# F4 and the overlay's "Jitter the projection" switch flip it live in either mode. Doing that while
# looking at the main menu is how a shimmer caused by the jitter is told apart from one caused by
# the resolve failing on elements that carry no motion vectors.
RSF_ENABLE_JITTER=1

# Name the game's pipeline objects and classify the draws made from them. Changes no pixel: it
# answers which draws on a screen are the interface and, more usefully, how many looked like it and
# matched no rule. Look for the "ui:" lines in the report.
#
# The creation hooks are only patched when this is on, so turning it off costs the game nothing.
RSF_UI_CLASSIFY=1

# Which transfer function the composite applies to the extracted interface. 0 none, 1 sRGB, 2 gamma
# 2.2.
#
# AC7 stores its interface as linear values in a plain UNORM target, measured: the "ui draw:" trace
# shows the widget quads writing into view format 28. The back buffer they are composited onto holds
# colour the game has already transformed for display, so the composite has to transform too, and
# without it the interface arrives dark.
#
# Which curve is right is not something the API records. 1 and 2 differ in the shadows: if 1 leaves
# dark parts of the interface slightly washed out, try 2.
RSF_UI_ENCODE=1

# Move each interface panel back by the jitter of the view it is drawn with, read from that view's
# own uniform buffer, so panels drawn with a jittered projection into a layer the reconstruction
# never sees hold still, and panels of unjittered views are left alone. 0 turns it off.
RSF_UI_UNJITTER=1

# Jitter every view rather than only the main one. 1 restores the plain gate patch. The main-view
# stub exists because scene-in-scene views, the briefing hologram and the menu backgrounds, took
# the jitter too and nothing resolved it.
# RSF_JITTER_ALL_VIEWS=0

# Name shaders the rules got wrong, by hash, in either direction. Forced wins over every rule and
# skipped loses to none, so this corrects a run without waiting for a build.
#
# The hashes come from the "ui draw:" trace lines, which print the vertex and pixel shader hash of
# the first two dozen interface-shaped draws for exactly this purpose. Hex, separated by anything.
#
# RSF_UI_SHADER_FORCE=0x1a2b3c4d, 0x5e6f7a8b
# RSF_UI_SHADER_SKIP=0x9c0d1e2f

# Render scale, as a percentage. The panel can change this at runtime.
RSF_SCREEN_PERCENTAGE=50

# 0 Native, 1 Quality, 2 Balanced, 3 Performance, 4 Ultra Performance.
RSF_DLSS_QUALITY=3

# Where dumps, logs and captures are written. A Windows path as Proton sees it.
RSF_DUMP_DIR=Z:\home\n1ght\Projekte\ReScaleFrame\.local\observe

# Left unset on purpose: the vendor runtime is found at ReScaleFrame\streamline beside the proxy.
# RSF_STREAMLINE_BIN=

# Frame capture, off unless asked for, and not a default worth having. RenderDoc wraps the device,
# NGX then refuses to create the DLSS feature, and the retry costs the game a little more every
# frame until it stops. Turn it on for a capture session, off for anything else. When on,
# renderdoc.dll is found beside the proxy; the path is only for a copy kept elsewhere.
# RSF_RENDERDOC=1
# RSF_RENDERDOC_DLL=
# Where captures are written, as RenderDoc's file path template: the frame number and .rdc are
# appended. Unset, RenderDoc picks its own temporary folder.
# RSF_CAPTURE_PREFIX=D:/captures/ac7

# Let translucent geometry write motion vectors. Stock 4.18 excludes it by blend mode, which is why
# the mission map's relief and the vehicle symbols in replay have none. The material domain check,
# the movable test and SupportsVelocity are all left in place, so a material with no usable velocity
# permutation still refuses rather than drawing wrongly.
#
# Off by default: separate translucency no longer goes through DLSS, so its motion would only
# overwrite the motion of the scene behind it.
#
# Check the log for: translucent velocity gate patched at rva 0x11823de
RSF_TRANSLUCENT_VELOCITY=0

# What reinsertion (F6) does when a promoted target meets the game's own depth.
#
# Reinsertion promotes the composite to output resolution. The game keeps binding a depth at render
# resolution, and D3D11 rejects that pair, so every draw in such a pass is dropped. Flat interface
# draws bind no depth and are untouched, which is how the interface can look right over a scene that
# is missing. All three answers are wrong in different ways, so pick by looking:
#
#   0  drop the depth and keep the substitution. The pass draws, without its depth test or writes.
#   1  forward both. The pair is invalid and the pass draws nothing. This is what it did before.
#   2  leave the target alone. The pass draws correctly into a texture nothing downstream reads.
#
# The count and the last mismatching pair are in the F7 report. Zero mismatches means depth is not
# involved and the missing geometry has another cause.
# RSF_REINSERT_DEPTH=0

# Separate translucency resolution.
#
# 4.18 renders that layer at half the scene and doubles it back on composite, so at a 50% render
# scale the mission map's relief is a quarter resolution image by the time anything sees it. The
# proxy patches the halving out; these decide what it renders at instead.
#
# The target is a percentage of the presented resolution, not of the scene, and it defaults to 100:
# the layer renders at native whatever the render scale. It does not go through DLSS. The bridge
# evaluates the reconstruction at the game's recombine, before translucency is composited, and the
# recombine lays the native layer over the reconstruction, drawn with the unjittered view. 0 means
# "match the scene" and puts the layer back at render resolution, jitter and all.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_TARGET=100

# A direct multiplier in percent of the scene, overriding the target when it is not zero.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_SCALE=0

# Going above the scene's resolution needs the engine to build a depth to match, which stock 4.18
# does only below 1.0: at or above it the layer borrows the scene's depth, and a large colour target
# with a small depth is a pair D3D11 rejects. Game-tested, that made the briefing relief vanish
# entirely. The proxy narrows that borrow to exactly 1.0 with four one-byte patches, after which the
# engine allocates, fills, view-transforms and resolves the layer's own depth at any size. There is
# no setting for it: if those patches do not match, the scale holds at the scene's resolution by
# itself and says so in the log.
